                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game is really good. As you might guess from the title, this is a pretty direct continuation of the first game, Mutant Mudds. It plays identically, and there aren't any new gameplay additions. I almost expected less, as I figured the subtitle \"Super Challenge\" would mean this is just a level pack, but it's a bit more than that. The biggest addition is the boss fights. The original Mutant Mudds had no boss fights; the game was just over after you'd collected everything. Now, to progress, you must defeat the five bosses of each world. For me, this is a welcome change, since it's what I'd wanted most from the first game. What's a difficult platformer without some nasty bosses, right? The other main improvements are on the side content. The graphics of the levels are that bit better (though Max is just like always), and there are some really gorgeous environments that I definitely don't remember seeing in the original. You can now officially collect music from the game's soundtrack and listen to it at your leisure. This includes tracks from the first game that (as far as I remember) didn't play in the second game. Even really small details are given extra polish, like the animation for the game over screen wiping in, and a sound effect playing for collecting the hundredth diamond in a level. There are also now many more characters to unlock. The first game had five: Grannie and the main devs of Renegade Kid, who all had every power at once. Now, you can find all sorts of characters while strolling around the secret levels. The most surprising part to me was that most of these characters are from other games! I was really shocked at a good few well-known games that had official representation in this game (though it might help that the first game was relatively popular on the 3DS). These characters are also very well-made and respect their source material.\r\n\r\nI am also very pleased with the difficulty of this game. The original game got pretty difficult in places, but even the hardest challenges were relatively tame from my perspective. This game starts with that difficulty. Even the first level of this game feels like it could have been one of the later ones in the first game, and that difficulty continues all the way through, as you would expect out of a game with the subtitle \"Super Challenge\". Of course, this difficulty isn't for everyone. I am decently good at video games owing to my NES experience, so I have the patience for a game that admittedly has some cheap and frustrating hazards.\r\n\r\nThe music of this game is great. Just like the original, all the tracks and sound effects are made as though on an audio chip like you'd see on an NES, though \"12-bit,\" as the advertising describes, is the best way I can put it. There are some effects that would otherwise be impossible on something like an NES.\r\n\r\nOverall, I highly recommend this game for people who want a challenge and some great gameplay. This is a sequel to Mutant Mudds in all the ways a fan of the first game would want. Just about everything in this game continues or improves upon the original, which itself was damn great. I can perhaps see a fan of the first game not enjoying the new bosses, but everything else is just like the first game, only better.",
